WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:18.080
Type 1 diabetes is a condition in which your pancreas does not produce insulin, a hormone

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.700
your body needs to maintain proper blood sugar levels.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.640
After you eat foods that contain carbohydrates,

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:34.960
chemicals in your small intestine break them down into single sugar molecules called glucose.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.080
Next, the cells lining your small intestine

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.360
absorb the glucose,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.260
which passes into the bloodstream.

00:00:46.159 --> 00:00:54.059
When the blood reaches your pancreas, beta cells inside the pancreas detect the rising glucose levels.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.920
The beta cells release insulin into your bloodstream

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:05.030
to reduce glucose levels and to keep your blood glucose in a healthy range.

00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:13.989
Most cells of the body have certain receptors on their surface that bind to the circulating insulin.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.520
Insulin acts like a key in a lock

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.940
to open up the cell so that the circulating glucose can get inside the cell.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:31.550
Now, your cell can use the glucose to produce the energy it needs to function properly.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:40.340
If you have type 1 diabetes, your pancreatic beta cells lose their ability to produce insulin,

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.460
resulting in high blood glucose levels and other complications.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:55.640
In type 1 diabetes, your immune system, specifically your white blood cells, mistake your pancreatic

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:02.560
beta cells for foreign invaders. In an autoimmune response, your white blood

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:08.490
cells secrete autoantibodies that destroy your own beta cells.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:14.130
As a result, your pancreas produces little or no insulin.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.760
Without insulin, glucose cannot get into your cells, so they are starved for the calories

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.450
they should be receiving from glucose.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.900
In addition, the glucose level builds up in

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:35.370
your bloodstream, resulting in a condition
called hyperglycemia.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.920
Common symptoms of hyperglycemia in type 1
diabetes include:

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.700
excessive hunger;

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.040
excessive thirst;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.300
frequent urination;

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.020
unexplained weight loss;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:54.640
fatigue;

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.160
weakness;

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:57.860
irritability;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:02:59.880
and blurry vision.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:08.040
If hyperglycemia is not treated, you can become severely ill.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:15.060
Because you don't have enough insulin circulating in your blood, your cells can't use glucose for energy.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.800
As a result, your body breaks down your fat

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.120
and protein stores as an alternative source of energy.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:31.760
As fat breakdown continues, certain byproducts, known as ketone bodies, accumulate in the blood,

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.820
resulting in a condition called ketosis.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.800
When ketones build up to dangerously high levels,

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:46.380
a life-threatening condition called diabetic ketoacidosis results.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:54.800
If your blood glucose remains high over time, long term health problems can occur, such as:

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.400
atherosclerosis;

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.220
blindness;

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:01.400
nerve damage;

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.100
and kidney disease.

00:04:06.099 --> 00:04:12.939
If you have type 1 diabetes, your goal is to keep your blood glucose within a normal range.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.219
This is done through a combination of proper insulin replacement,

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:20.399
monitoring your blood glucose,

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:26.700
and, just as importantly, eating a healthy diet and getting regular exercise.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:33.260
Because your pancreas no longer produces insulin,

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:38.409
you will need to take insulin to replace what your body should be making.

00:04:38.409 --> 00:04:44.589
A licensed healthcare professional can train you to inject the insulin just under the skin.

00:04:45.379 --> 00:04:49.319
You will need to give yourself injections several times each day,

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:56.120
and rotate injection sites to avoid tissue damage and absorption problems.

00:04:56.129 --> 00:05:02.169
Another way to get insulin is through an insulin pump, which is attached to your body

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.800
and delivers insulin through a tube implanted just under your skin.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:15.250
You will need to check the level of glucose in your blood several times a day with a glucometer.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:20.520
To do this, you will prick your finger with a small needle called a lancet,

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.120
and place a drop of blood in the glucometer.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.080
Knowledge of your blood glucose level allows you to

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:38.080
adjust your insulin dose, calories you eat during meals, and physical activity.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.699
You will need to eat a healthy diet and get
regular exercise to manage your glucose level

00:05:46.819 --> 00:05:50.319
and reduce your risk of cardiovascular disease.

